00:12:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand where you're coming from—it feels like electricity is just a cleaner, cheaper “fuel” all around, and if it saves you money in one place, it ought to do the same elsewhere. The thing is, electricity isn’t actually a fundamentally cheaper *form* of energy in the way we pay for it. Let me explain why, without formulas, just by looking at what a dollar buys you in each case. ### 1. Electricity isn't a fuel—it's a *way* of delivering energy Gasoline, natural gas, and coal are fuels you can burn directly. Electricity is a carrier: it’s made from those fuels (or wind/solar) and sent to you. Each time we convert energy from one form to another, we lose a bit. When you burn a fuel at a power plant, you get electricity, but about half the original energy is lost as heat. That loss is baked into the price per kilowatt-hour. By contrast, burning natural gas in your furnace skips that middle step, so you get the full benefit of the fuel’s heat right in your home. So asking if electricity is “cheaper” is like asking if a smoothie is cheaper than just eating the fruit. The smoothie might be more convenient and tastier, but you paid for someone to blend it, package it, and deliver it. ### 2. A dollar’s worth of each energy source gives very different amounts of *what you actually need* - **For driving:** Gas engines are so wasteful (75% lost) that even though a gallon of gas may contain a lot of energy, most of it doesn’t move the car. An EV’s electric motor uses the energy so efficiently that a little electricity goes a long way. That’s why your neighbor saves money—the *useful work* per dollar is much higher. - **For heating:** The job is just making heat. A gas furnace already does that with 95% efficiency. So when you spend $1 on gas, you get nearly $1 worth of heat in your home. When you spend $1 on electricity for resistance heat, you get $1 worth of heat too—but because the electricity itself is more expensive per unit of energy (thanks to generation/transmission losses), you end up paying more for the same warmth. Let me put real numbers to it: In the U.S., a typical residential price for natural gas is about $1.50 per 100,000 BTU. For electricity at $0.13/kWh, that same 100,000 BTU costs about $3.80. That’s just the market price—electricity is simply *priced higher* for an equivalent amount of raw energy. ### 3. The only reason the “cheaper” feeling exists is efficiency differences If we had 100% efficient gasoline cars and 100% efficient electric cars, the cost comparison would be purely about fuel price per unit of energy. Then we’d say, “Gas is cheaper per BTU than electricity, so gas cars would be cheaper to run.” But we don’t have that—electricity wins only because it’s used in an incredibly efficient motor. With heating, we’re already near the efficiency ceiling for both gas and electric resistance. There’s no secret efficiency gain to unlock. So the cost differences come straight from the commodity prices: per unit of heat, natural gas is usually cheaper than electricity. That’s not a formula; it’s just your utility bill. ### 4. Heat pumps are the exception that proves the rule A heat pump can *deliver more heat* than the electricity it consumes (coefficient of performance of 3 means 3 units of heat for 1 unit of electricity). That makes it competitive with gas on an operating cost basis. But notice: it’s not because electricity itself is cheap; it’s because the device wrings out more heat from every watt. If electricity were inherently a cheaper energy source, a plain old baseboard heater would beat gas—but it doesn’t, in almost any market. ### 5. Think of it like buying milk vs. buying coffee A gallon of milk might cost $3, and a gallon of coffee might cost $10. If you only need a cup to wake up, coffee is a better deal because a little goes a long way (efficiency of purpose). But if you’re filling a bathtub with something white, milk is the cheaper option. Cars need high-quality mechanical energy; houses just need raw heat. The “cost per unit of final service” is what matters, not the source. Now, I’m not dismissing what your installer said. They may have been referring to a heat pump, or you may live in an area where electric rates are unusually low and gas is expensive. In those rare cases, electric resistance can indeed be cheaper. But as a general rule, for heating, electricity isn’t the economy choice—it’s the efficiency of the *appliance* that makes or breaks the deal. I know this feels backward because the car example is so striking. The car is saving money due to technology leapfrogging a century-old engine’s wastefulness. In the basement furnace, there’s no equivalent waste to eliminate.
