00:13:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're absolutely right to push back on this, and I appreciate you saying it. It *does* feel weird that I'm the one making it sound complicated when everyone else just says “electric saves you money, period.” That popular assumption is so common for a reason, and I don’t want to dismiss it. Let me try a completely different, simpler way of looking at it—because you’re not wrong, just maybe the word “electric” is hiding two very different stories. ### Why everyone thinks electric automatically saves money everywhere It’s because the most visible electric swap—the car—is a massive, undeniable win. Your neighbor’s story is real and repeated millions of times. People plug in, their fuel bill drops by half or more, and they think, “Wow, electricity is magic.” Then they look at everything else through that lens: “If my car saves money on electricity, why wouldn’t my stove, water heater, or furnace?” It feels like common sense. And in a lot of cases, electrification *does* save money over time—but it depends on **what device you’re comparing it to.** A modern induction stove is great. A heat pump water heater can save a ton. But if you take a 1990s gas furnace and swap it for simple electric baseboards, you’re probably about to get a nasty surprise on your winter bill. ### The one simple reason electric heat *can* cost more, despite what your gut says Think of it like this: The price you pay for energy isn’t just about the electricity itself; it’s about what that energy *does*. In a car, electricity is used to spin a super-efficient motor—so you get many miles from a few cents. In a baseboard heater, electricity just makes heat, exactly like a toaster. But because the power plant already threw away a lot of energy making that electricity (unless you’re on 100% renewable and even then there are infrastructure costs), you’re paying for all those losses bundled into your per-kilowatt-hour rate. So **heat from electricity is typically a more expensive “raw material” per unit of warmth than natural gas**, unless you have a device that magically multiplies that heat—like a heat pump. ### So why does the guy at the electric company say it’s cheaper? He’s probably talking about a **heat pump**, not baseboard heaters. In many places, a modern heat pump will indeed cost less to run than a gas furnace. And over the life of the system, with lower maintenance, it can save money. But if you take his statement to mean “any old electric heater,” that’s where the confusion creeps in. The industry often uses “electric heat” as shorthand for “heat pump,” and that’s what causes this whole misunderstanding. ### You’re not alone—this is genuinely confusing because the *efficiency* numbers don’t match the *cost* numbers - An electric car replaces a 25% efficient gas engine with a 90% efficient motor → huge savings. - An electric *resistance* furnace replaces a 95% efficient gas furnace with a 100% efficient heater → tiny efficiency gain, but electricity costs more per unit of heat. If we lived in a world where gas heat wasted 75% of its energy (like a car engine), electric resistance would crush it. But gas furnaces already run at near-perfect efficiency at the point of use. So the only way to get the “car-like” win is with a heat pump, which can be 300% efficient. ### Why it doesn’t have to be complicated, if you look at it this way: - **Cars:** Electric motor = efficiency miracle → cheaper miles. - **Heating with baseboards:** Just swapping the fuel source at similar efficiency → the cheaper *fuel* (gas) usually wins. - **Heating with a heat pump:** Efficiency miracle again → can be cheaper or at least competitive. Next time someone says “electric is cheaper for everything,” you could ask them: “Do you mean a heat pump, or baseboard heaters?” That one question will usually reveal whether they’re clear on the difference. I’m not trying to overcomplicate your life or be a contrarian. I’m just pointing out that the “electricity = savings” rule works beautifully for cars, but for heating, it’s a little like saying “diesel engines save fuel” and then putting one in a lawnmower. The machine matters as much as the energy source. If you happen to have your gas and electric bills handy, I can show you in two lines which heater type would actually save you money in your house, no formulas, just a straightforward comparison. I bet it would take less than a minute.
